  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM EDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nThere's really not much to talk about for much of the coming week. \nAn upper ridge extends from Texas into the Great Lakes region this \nmorning, with an upper low and associated Nor'easter off the \nnortheast coastline this morning. This basic central CONUS ridge and \neastern CONUS trough pattern, holds through early next week before \nthe ridge shifts to the Appalachian chain and southeast CONUS \nWednesday through the end of the week and an upper low settles over \nthe plains. \n\nAs far as sensible weather goes locally, pleasant fall temperatures \ncontinue through Monday, with a notable warming trend expected \nTuesday onward as the ridge builds overhead. Highs extending several \ndegrees above normal look likely by the latter half of the week. Dry \nconditions should prevail through much of the coming week. The \nplains upper low looks to open up and eject ENE sometime Thursday \nnight into Friday, with associated broad SSW low level flow pulling \nmoisture northward from the Gulf into the greater Tennessee valley \nand southern Appalachian region. Current NBM guidance brings rain \nchances back into the mix in earnest by Friday. While that's \nplausible, there's enough disagreement in guidance to not buy into \nrain chances that far out just yet, plus experience suggests these \ntypes of patterns break down slower than expected. All of that to \nsay, maybe check the forecast in the coming days and don't bank of \nnext weekend being a washout just yet.",
